// A file left in the middle of a merge: each conflict is one error, at its `<<<<<<<` line,
// and nothing in it is reported as broken syntax. The first side is checked as the file's
// text, so the rest of the file's errors are still found, and the second side isn't read.
//# write a.ctx:
//| fn main { mut io: Io } {
//| <<<<<<< HEAD
//|     let n: u8 = 3
//| =======
//|     let n: u8 = "four" +
//| >>>>>>> theirs
//|     io::println_i64{ &io, n }
//| }
//| <<<<<<< HEAD
//| fn small {} -> u8 { return 300 }
//| ||||||| base
//| fn small {} -> u8 { return 3 }
//| =======
//| fn small {} -> u16 { return 300 }
//| >>>>>>> theirs
//# $ ctxc check {dir}/a.ctx
//# exit: 1
//# stderr:
//| {dir}/a.ctx:2:1: error: unresolved merge conflict, to the `>>>>>>>` at line 6: keep one side and delete the marker lines (the first side was checked, and the rest skipped)
//| {dir}/a.ctx:9:1: error: unresolved merge conflict, to the `>>>>>>>` at line 15: keep one side and delete the marker lines (the first side was checked, and the rest skipped)
//| {dir}/a.ctx:10:28: error: literal 300 does not fit in u8
//| check: 3 errors; not checked until the program has no other error: the order of a call's fields, what tasks borrow, consts computed by running code

